<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Wey</h1><p class="st-meta">1040 BCE – 209 BCE (approx.) · capital Zhaoge, Diqiu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Ji-clan state planted in the old Shang heartland that outlived every other Zhou state, surviving as a single city until 209 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the rebellion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rebellion-of-the-three-guards">Three Guards</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a> gave the former <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang</a> capital region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhaoge">Zhaoge</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangshu-feng">Kang Shu Feng</a>, a younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou">King Wu</a>, to watch over the conquered Shang population. Wey is spelt so in English to distinguish it from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-wei">State of Wei</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Wey was a large and cultured state early in the Spring and Autumn period, and its folk songs make up a large part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Book of Songs</a>. In 660 BCE the Di overran it after Duke Yi, notorious for giving his cranes official carriages, was killed. Rebuilt with help from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi</a>, it moved to Chuqiu and then Diqiu (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyang">Puyang</a>). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> spent years there, and the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shang-yang">Shang Yang</a> was a Wey nobleman.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Wey shrank to the area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyang">Puyang</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> first of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-wei">State of Wei</a> and then of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a>, which spared it even after unification. In 209 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-er-shi">Second Emperor of Qin</a> deposed Lord Jue and made him a commoner, ending the last Zhou state.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-wey.jpg" alt="State of Wey" width="320" height="217"><figcaption>Image: Yug · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinese_plain_5c._BC-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1040 BCE – 209 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zhaoge, Diqiu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kang Shu Feng, Duke Yi of Wey, Lord Jue of Wey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wey, Wei (Zhou state)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-624&amp;focus=state-of-wey" aria-label="State of Wey on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7565 891 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Wey lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7885 1147L7768 1193M7885 1147L7768 1187M7885 1147L7768 1187M7885 1147L7868 1099"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1193h0M7768 1187h0M7768 1187h0M7868 1099h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7885 1147h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7885 1147h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zhaoge. <a href="/atlas?year=-624&amp;focus=state-of-wey">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

